rav1d_pin_66f58fa6 / 2026-08-29
Exposure measurement for moving this workspace's rav1d-safe from
140f91450c35 -> 66f58fa6a64c (96 commits), taken BEFORE the pin landed.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY PINNED, AND WHERE (the drift this fixes)
  Three revs were live across the zen workspace: zenavif on 66f58fa6 (its
  current main), ravif on a6a7e232, and this workspace resolving 140f9145.
  The `[patch.crates-io] rav1d-safe = { … rev = "f6aed27e…" }` entry in
  Cargo.toml was NOT the pin — it was dead. zenavif had stopped taking
  rav1d-safe from crates.io (it is a direct git dep on zenavif's dependency
  line now), so nothing in this graph wanted rav1d-safe from the registry and
  Cargo recorded the entry as `[[patch.unused]]`. The real rev came from
  whichever ../zenavif was checked out — locally the sibling, in CI the pinned
  clone in .github/workflows/ci.yml, and those two had drifted apart.
  A git-source patch cannot fix that either: `[patch."https://github.com/
  imazen/rav1d-safe"]` pointing at the same URL with a different rev is
  REJECTED — measured, `cargo metadata` exits 101 with "patch for `rav1d-safe`
  points to the same source, but patches must point to different sources"
  (Cargo compares patch sources by URL; `?rev=` is not part of that identity).
  So the CI zenavif clone rev IS this repo's rav1d-safe pin, and that is what
  this change moves (6e69831d -> b186cb8, i.e. 140f9145 -> 66f58fa6).

WHERE rav1d-safe IS COMPILED (and where it is not)
  NO CI JOB COMPILES IT. Every job in ci.yml runs `--no-default-features` with
  a feature set that excludes `avif`; verified with `cargo tree -e normal` —
  `cuda,all-metrics` (Compile), `wgpu,all-metrics` (Lint + Doctests) and
  `cpu-metrics` (CLI build) all resolve WITHOUT zenavif or rav1d-safe in the
  graph. CI only has to RESOLVE the ../zenavif path, never build it.
  The PRODUCTION fleet binary does compile it: the executor image is built
  `--features sweep,png,jpeg,webp,avif,jxl,cpu-metrics` for
  x86_64-unknown-linux-musl (scripts/jobsys/build_executor_image.sh:15). That
  is exactly the platform rav1d-safe#524 affects, and the AVIF decode reached
  by `zenavif::decode_with` (crates/zenmetrics-cli/src/decode.rs:216) and
  `ManagedAvifDecoder` (hdr.rs:175, sweep/hdr.rs:888) during a sweep.
  So: this bump is a PRODUCTION fix with NO CI coverage. Do not read a green
  CI run as evidence about the decoder.

INSTRUMENT
  `zenmetrics score --metric ssim2 --reference F --distorted F` over every
  AVIF in ../codec-corpus (140 files: 106 avif-conformance/valid, 19
  edge-cases, 12 invalid, 3 elsewhere). Self-comparison, so a decode that
  succeeds scores exactly 100.000000 and the interesting output is the
  accept/reject verdict plus the error text. Release build,
  `--no-default-features --features avif,cpu-metrics`.

  SINGLE-VARIABLE DESIGN. zenavif is held FIXED at b186cb8 in both arms; only
  the rav1d-safe rev differs. The old arm builds against a scratch clone of
  zenavif b186cb8 whose ONLY delta is its rav1d-safe rev line (140f9145 vs
  66f58fa6) — `git diff --stat` on that clone is `Cargo.toml | 2 +-`. This
  avoids confounding the decoder change with the two days of zenavif changes
  between the old and new CI clone revs.

RESULT — one verdict changes, and it is a deliberately-corrupt vector
  arm  rav1d-safe   ACCEPT  REJECT
  old  140f91450c35    124      16
  new  66f58fa6a64c    123      17

  Set diff over all 140 files — exactly one row moves:
    avif-conformance/invalid/corrupted_mdat.avif
      old: ACCEPT, ssim2 = 100.000000
      new: REJECT, "zenavif: Malformed AVIF: Failed to decode primary frame"

  Cause: rav1d-safe@2e0f7e8 made `Settings::strictness` default to
  `Strictness::Strict`, so non-conforming streams are `Error::InvalidData`
  instead of being concealed. zenmetrics never names `Settings` — it inherits
  the default through zenavif's `DecoderConfig` — so it takes the new
  behaviour automatically. This reproduces zenavif's own survey (315
  AVIFs/OBUs, identical verdicts except this same file) and upstream's
  53,484-AVIF sweep (0 disagreements).

  RECOMMENDATION: KEEP STRICT. Do not set `Strictness::Lenient` here. The old
  behaviour scored a file in `avif-conformance/invalid/` as a PERFECT
  ssim2 = 100.000000 — a metrics harness silently emitting a perfect score for
  a corrupt input is the exact failure mode this repo exists to avoid, and any
  such row would have been indistinguishable from a real one in a sweep
  parquet. Rejecting it is the correct outcome, and the reject is loud.

  The other 16 rejects are unchanged across both arms and are zenavif-level
  container/policy refusals, not decoder strictness: 4 grid-with-alpha
  unsupported, 3 bad ftyp brand, 2 ftyp not first, 1 NCL matrix_coefficients
  refusal, and 6 assorted parse errors (tmap version, missing pitm, zero ispe,
  box size mismatch, …).

DECODED PIXELS DO NOT MOVE
  On all 123 files accepted by BOTH arms, the result text is byte-identical
  (every one `metric=ssim2 ssim2=100.000000`). Independently corroborated on
  the encoder side in imazen/ravif's
  benchmarks/rav1d_pin_66f58fa6_2026-08-29.tsv.meta, which bisected the same
  range on 400 sweep cells: 140f9145 -> 66f58fa6 moves 0/400 SSIMULACRA2
  scores with encoded bytes held fixed. (That measurement also found that the
  EARLIER range a6a7e232 -> 140f9145 does move aarch64 pixels — the 2026-08-07
  NEON conformance campaign that took rav1d-safe from 302/766 to 766/766
  against dav1d's MD5s. This workspace was already past that point, so it is
  not in scope here; ravif was not, and took it in the same wave.)

flush()
  `Decoder::flush()` draining owed frames before it resets (rav1d-safe@59eb17b,
  rav1d-safe#423) does not reach zenmetrics: every `.flush()` in this workspace
  is `std::io::Write::flush`. zenavif calls it, in decode_av1.rs and
  decoder_managed/decoder.rs, where it strictly recovers frames that were
  previously dropped — it cannot lose one. Still-image AVIF decode returns a
  single frame from one `decode()` call, so there is nothing owed to drain in
  the common path here anyway.

WHAT LANDED WITH THIS
  * .github/workflows/ci.yml — the ../zenavif clone rev, all five copies of
    the "Clone sibling-repo path-dep targets" step, 6e69831d -> b186cb8, with
    a comment at each saying that this pin IS the rav1d-safe pin.
  * Cargo.toml — the dead `[patch.crates-io] rav1d-safe` entry removed and
    replaced by a note recording why neither a crates-io nor a git-source
    patch can pin it from here.
  * Cargo.lock — rav1d-safe + rav1d-disjoint-mut 140f9145 -> 66f58fa6, and the
    `[[patch.unused]] rav1d-safe 0.5.7 @ f6aed27e` stanza drops out, which is
    the proof the old entry was inert. (`[[patch.unused]] zenrav1e` remains; it
    was already unused on master and is unrelated.)

PROVENANCE
  zenmetrics     e248ea05 (master) + this commit
  zenavif        b186cb8b1e2624c96e16435247205f0b0e02b943  (both arms)
  rav1d-safe     140f91450c3551c25a5699d12ded6d629ecf6d97  (old arm)
                 66f58fa6a64c689998721cc5cdb16a4698e26eec  (new arm)
  corpus         ../codec-corpus, 140 .avif files
  rustc          1.98.0 (88d9e12ae 2026-08-18)
  host           Apple M4 Pro, arm64, macOS 26.5.2, 12 cores
  every build and run under `nice -n 19`
  NOTE ON ARCH: this host is aarch64. rav1d-safe#524's loop-filter defect is
  in the 8bpc x86_64 H kernels, so the crash it fixes CANNOT be exercised
  here. The accept/reject and decode-identity results above are
  architecture-independent (container parsing + strictness gating + the
  decoded output the metric consumes); the x86_64 crash fix is taken on
  upstream's evidence and zenavif's, not on a local run.

